---
title: Italian Sunday gravy
servings: 10
tags: [italian, pork, weekend, make-ahead]
cuisine: italian
time: 4 hours 20 minutes
source: Italian Sunday Gravy (Sugo) by Coley Cooks, https://coleycooks.com/authentic-italian-sunday-gravy
nutrition:
  calories: 421 kcal
  fat: 28 g
  saturated fat: 9 g
  carbohydrates: 9 g
  sugar: 5 g
  protein: 32 g
  fiber: 2 g
  sodium: 694 mg
  serving size: 1 serving
---

Season the @pork loin{2-3%lb} all over with @kosher salt{} and
@black pepper{}. Heat the @extra virgin olive oil{2%tbsp} in a #Dutch oven{}
over high heat and brown the pork on every side. Lift it out.

Turn the heat to medium. Cook the @yellow onion{1}(finely diced) ~{5%minutes}
until soft.

Add the @garlic{5%cloves}(thinly sliced) and a pinch of
@red pepper flakes{}. Cook ~{2%minutes}, then stir in the
@tomato paste{3%tbsp} for ~{1-2%minutes}.

Add the @whole peeled tomatoes{56%oz}(two 28 oz cans; or crushed tomatoes),
@bay leaves{2} and @Italian parsley{1%tbsp}(finely chopped). Season. Half to
three-quarters fill one empty can with @water{}, swirl, and add it. Break up
whole tomatoes with a wooden spoon, or blend for a smoother sauce.

Bring to a boil. Add the pork, the @Italian sausage{1.5%lb}(hot, sweet or both) and the @meatballs{16-20}(cooked, homemade or bought). Turn the heat to
the lowest setting.

Simmer with the lid ajar ~{3%hours}, stirring every ~{20%minutes} so the bottom
does not catch, until the pork falls apart. Let the meat sit in the sauce until
you eat.

Taste for salt. Remove the bay leaves and any bones, and lift the meat onto a
platter.

Toss al dente @pasta{} with a little of the sauce. Serve the rest of the sauce
and the meat alongside, with @Pecorino Romano{}(grated) and
@ricotta cheese{} at the table.
